<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Taj Mahal</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Taj Mahal is a white marble mausoleum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, built by the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a> in the seventeenth century in memory of his wife Mumtaz Mahal. Combining Persian, Central Asian and Indian architectural traditions, it is widely regarded as the masterpiece of Mughal architecture. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> cite it as part of the empire's enduring cultural legacy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/taj-mahal.jpg" alt="Taj Mahal" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yann; edited by Jim Carter · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Taj_Mahal_(Edited).jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a>, the fifth Mughal emperor, commissioned the Taj Mahal in 1631 as a tomb for his wife Mumtaz Mahal, who died on 17 June that year giving birth to their 14th child. Court historians record that he avoided royal affairs for a week in his grief and for two years gave up music and lavish dress. He chose land south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a> on which stood a mansion of Raja <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jai-singh-i">Jai Singh I</a>, who agreed to give it in exchange for a large palace in the centre of the city.</p><p>Construction began in 1632. The mausoleum was completed in 1648 and the rest of the complex in 1653. More than 20,000 workers and artisans took part under a board of architects led by the court architect Ustad Ahmad Lahori. The white marble came from Makrana in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>, and 28 kinds of precious and semi-precious stone, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> among other places, were inlaid in it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The mausoleum stands on the right bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a> at the centre of a complex of 17 hectares, with a mosque, a guest house and formal gardens enclosed by a crenellated red sandstone wall on three sides. It draws on Timurid and Mughal models, including the Gur-e Amir in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>, the tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humayuns-tomb">Humayun's Tomb</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, which inspired its charbagh garden.</p><p>The marble building stands on a square plinth with four identical sides, each with an iwan, a vaulted arched doorway, and is topped by an onion-shaped marble dome 23 metres high with a gilded finial. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a> himself was later buried beside his wife. The plan is symmetrical and aligned north to south, and the marble appears to change colour with the hour and the weather.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a>, deposed by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> in 1658, was held at Agra Fort, from where he could see the Taj Mahal, and died in 1666. By the late 19th century parts were in disrepair, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-curzon">Lord Curzon</a> ordered a restoration completed in 1908. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archaeological-survey-of-india">Archaeological Survey of India</a> has maintained it since 1947.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> designated it a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 1983 as the jewel of Muslim art in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and one of the universally admired masterpieces of the world's heritage. A longstanding story that he planned a black marble twin across the river comes from the writings of the traveller Jean-Baptiste Tavernier, and excavations in the 1990s showed that the blackened stones at Mehtab Bagh had been white.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Taj Mahal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mughal patronage of art, architecture and literature produced Humayun’s Tomb, Fatehpur Sikri, the Red Fort and, most famously, the <b>Taj Mahal</b> built by Shah Jahan, all of which combine Persian, Indian and Islamic artistic traditions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Islamic architecture produced the Dome of the Rock in Jerusalem, completed in 691–692, the Great Mosque of Córdoba, the Alhambra of Granada, the mosques of Isfahan and Istanbul and the <b>Taj Mahal</b>.</blockquote><a href="/religion/islam">Islam · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Shah Jahan commissioned the <b>Taj Mahal</b> in Agra to house her tomb.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-17">17 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
